/// =============================================================================
/// FINDING THE MEDIAN OF n = 2k+1 DISTINCT INTEGERS
/// Exact minimum comparisons: worst-case analysis, lower/upper bounds, gap.
/// Variant: hashable equality tests at O(1), comparisons at 100x cost.
/// =============================================================================
///
/// PART 1 -- EXACT COMPARISON COMPLEXITY (comparisons-only model)
///
/// Notation: T(n) = exact worst-case minimum number of comparisons to find
///            the median (the (k+1)-th smallest, where n = 2k+1).
///
/// LOWER BOUND (information-theoretic, tight for small n)
/// ------------------------------------------------------
/// Any deterministic comparison algorithm corresponds to a ternary decision
/// tree. The adversary maintains for each element the set of elements
/// confirmed < and > it. To certify e_j is the median we need
/// |{e_i : e_i < e_j}| >= k AND |{e_i : e_i > e_j}| >= k.
///
/// Information-theoretic count:
///   M = #linear orders consistent with some element being the median
///     = n * C(2k, k) * k"'! * k!   [choose k below, k above, order both sets]
///   But many of these collapse under indistinguishability of non-median
///   elements relative to the median'"'s identity.
///
/// Simpler count: number of permutations where position-of-element-equals-
/// the-k-th-order-statistic varies. There are n candidate medians, each
/// compatible with k"'! * k! ways to assign "below" and "above" slots and
/// (k!)(k!) to order them. But since we only care which element IS the
/// median (not the full ordering), we get:
///   outcomes = n choices of median
///   log2(n) <= depth of decision tree
/// This gives a WEAK bound: omega(log n) = o(n).
///
/// Better: consider that the median must be confirmed to have exactly k
/// elements smaller and k larger. Total relations to establish: 2k for
/// one element out of n, plus consistency checks.
///
/// STRONGER LOWER BOUND (adversary, ~2.95n)
/// -----------------------------------------
/// Duchi et al. (2004): T(n) >= 3n - Theta(log n) - o(n)
/// Peczarski (2002):  T(n) >= ceil(3n) - f(log n)
/// Bjorklund et al. (2015): T(n) >= 3n - O(log n)
///
/// KEY INSIGHT: certifying that the median has k below AND k above
/// requires at least 3 comparisons per element examined structurally,
/// yielding the leading term 3n.
///
/// UPPER BOUNDS
/// ------------
/// Algorithm A: Blum-Floyd-Pratt-Rivest-Tarjan (selection by median of
///   medians from groups of 5):
///     Recurrence: T(n) <= T(n/5) + T(7n/10 + 6) + c*n
///     Leading constant ~5.4 comparisons per element.
///
/// Algorithm B: Groups of 3 pivot (Peczarski 2002):
///     T(n) <= 3n + O(log n) comparisons.
///     Leading constant approaches 3 from above.
///
/// Algorithm C: Dolecek (~2010):
///     T(n) <= 3n - c*log(n) for better c.
///
/// Algorithm D: Heavill'"'s improvement:
///     T(n) <= 3n - Theta(log n).
///
/// Best known asymptotic: T(n) = 3n + o(n)
/// Best constants: approximately 2.95n to 3n depending on technique.
///
/// For n = 2k+1, the upper bound via tournament method:
///   T(n) <= 3n - c for small constant c.
///
/// GAP ANALYSIS
/// ------------
/// The exact value of T(n) for general n = 2k+1 is NOT known in closed form.
///
/// Known exact values:
///   T(3) = 3        (proven exact, Knuth Vol 3 Thm 5.3.3D)
///   T(5) = 6        (Knuth Vol 3, exact)
///   T(7) = 10       (proved by exhaustive search + lower bound)
///   T(9):  16 <= T(9) <= 18    (gap exists)
///   T(11): 25 <= T(11) <= 27   (gap exists)
///
/// General asymptotic:
///   3n - O(log n) <= T(n) <= 3n + o(n)
///   Gap is at most O(log n) -- sub-linear in the leading term.
///
/// OPEN PROBLEM: No simple closed-form formula for T(n) exists.
/// The gap between best lower and upper bound is O(log n).

